WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.229
FundsXML in production: how do the files get from one system into another, every day, reliably?

2
00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:15.355
In this film you see who sends what to whom, the delivery chain on both sides, how files travel, and the building blocks every production setup needs.

3
00:00:16.605 --> 00:00:18.064
Start with the parties.

4
00:00:18.114 --> 00:00:26.465
The asset manager and the fund administrator produce most of the data: master data, prices, portfolios and regulatory templates.

5
00:00:26.515 --> 00:00:29.418
The custodian adds holdings and transactions.

6
00:00:29.468 --> 00:00:33.487
Platforms and distributors, insurers and data vendors receive.

7
00:00:33.537 --> 00:00:39.721
Many companies are on both sides: an insurer receives portfolios, and sends its own reports on.

8
00:00:41.071 --> 00:00:43.327
Each receiver needs a different part.

9
00:00:43.377 --> 00:00:46.754
Platforms and data vendors need prices and master data.

10
00:00:46.804 --> 00:00:51.615
Insurers need the full portfolio and the Tripartite Template for Solvency two.

11
00:00:51.665 --> 00:00:56.102
Distributors need the European MiFID and E S G templates, and documents.

12
00:00:56.152 --> 00:01:00.901
One sender therefore produces several kinds of deliveries from the same data.

13
00:01:02.251 --> 00:01:05.381
On the sender's side, the chain has five steps.

14
00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:11.672
Extract the data from the source systems: fund accounting, portfolio management, master data.

15
00:01:11.722 --> 00:01:13.905
Map it to the FundsXML structure.

16
00:01:13.955 --> 00:01:15.123
Generate the file.

17
00:01:15.173 --> 00:01:19.114
Validate it, against the schema and against business rules.

18
00:01:19.164 --> 00:01:21.279
And deliver it to the receivers.

19
00:01:22.629 --> 00:01:24.871
Here is the whole chain on one page.

20
00:01:24.921 --> 00:01:27.291
The data rarely comes from one system.

21
00:01:27.341 --> 00:01:34.932
Net asset values come from fund accounting, positions from the portfolio system, identifiers and names from master data.

22
00:01:34.982 --> 00:01:41.044
A good setup collects them in one place first, often a database, where the data is checked once.

23
00:01:41.094 --> 00:01:49.704
From there, a generator writes the file each partner needs, it is validated, and it travels by S F T P, through an A P I or to a portal.

24
00:01:51.054 --> 00:01:52.297
Why one central place?

25
00:01:52.347 --> 00:01:56.119
Because the data is checked once and then used for every partner.

26
00:01:56.169 --> 00:02:00.586
Corrections are made in one place, and the history shows what was sent when.

27
00:02:00.636 --> 00:02:09.052
And every partner gets the same numbers, which avoids the classic problem of two receivers with two different net asset values for the same day.

28
00:02:10.402 --> 00:02:13.145
Follow one net asset value through the chain.

29
00:02:13.195 --> 00:02:16.434
At six in the evening, fund accounting calculates it.

30
00:02:16.484 --> 00:02:17.986
At eight, it is approved.

31
00:02:18.036 --> 00:02:23.758
At ten, the file is generated and validated, and a quarter of an hour later it is on its way.

32
00:02:23.808 --> 00:02:29.717
At seven the next morning, the platform has loaded it, and by nine, investors see the new price.

33
00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:32.820
Every step has a time, and every step can be late.

34
00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:37.517
On the receiver's side, the chain mirrors the sender's.

35
00:02:37.567 --> 00:02:38.567
Receive the file.

36
00:02:38.617 --> 00:02:40.667
Validate it, before anything else.

37
00:02:40.717 --> 00:02:42.458
Load it into the own systems.

38
00:02:42.508 --> 00:02:46.966
Reconcile it: do the numbers fit together and with what you already know?

39
00:02:47.016 --> 00:02:51.166
And send feedback to the sender, especially when something is wrong.

40
00:02:52.516 --> 00:02:54.056
How does the file travel?

41
00:02:54.106 --> 00:02:56.790
Most often by S F T P, on a fixed schedule.

42
00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:59.206
More and more partners offer an A P I.

43
00:02:59.256 --> 00:03:03.021
Fund platforms and data vendors often run their own portals.

44
00:03:03.071 --> 00:03:09.189
E-mail is still common, but it is hard to automate and to monitor, so it should be the exception.

45
00:03:10.539 --> 00:03:13.574
With S F T P, a few rules prevent most problems.

46
00:03:13.624 --> 00:03:18.395
Agree on file names that contain the sender, the kind of data and the date.

47
00:03:18.445 --> 00:03:26.044
Upload under a temporary name and rename the file when it is complete, so that the receiver never picks up half a file.

48
00:03:26.094 --> 00:03:27.394
Compress large files.

49
00:03:27.444 --> 00:03:30.544
And use keys per partner, never shared passwords.

50
00:03:31.894 --> 00:03:35.711
Fund data is often confidential, portfolios in particular.

51
00:03:35.761 --> 00:03:37.711
Transport is always encrypted.

52
00:03:37.761 --> 00:03:44.244
Where proof of origin matters, a FundsXML file can carry an XML signature; the schema supports it.

53
00:03:44.294 --> 00:03:47.577
Every file sent and received goes into an archive.

54
00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:52.444
And access to the data is limited to the people and systems that need it.

55
00:03:53.794 --> 00:03:56.092
Different data has a different rhythm.

56
00:03:56.142 --> 00:04:02.952
Prices and net asset values go out daily, often with an agreed cut-off time, for example by ten in the morning.

57
00:04:03.002 --> 00:04:04.744
Portfolios are often monthly.

58
00:04:04.794 --> 00:04:09.193
Master data, documents and regulatory templates go out when they change.

59
00:04:09.243 --> 00:04:12.159
And corrections go out whenever they are needed.

60
00:04:12.209 --> 00:04:15.620
Each delivery is its own file, with its own document ID.

61
00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:19.617
Not every partner uses the same schema version.

62
00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:25.012
A sender often writes two or three versions at the same time, one for each group of receivers.

63
00:04:25.062 --> 00:04:34.366
That needs a clear list of which partner gets which version and which templates, tests for every version, and a plan for moving partners to a new version together.

64
00:04:35.716 --> 00:04:40.114
Whatever the tools, every production setup needs five building blocks.

65
00:04:40.164 --> 00:04:43.100
A scheduler that starts the deliveries on time.

66
00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:44.688
Validation on both sides.

67
00:04:44.738 --> 00:04:47.421
An archive of every file sent and received.

68
00:04:47.471 --> 00:04:50.788
Monitoring that notices when a file is late or wrong.

69
00:04:50.838 --> 00:04:53.838
And a partner register that knows who gets what.

70
00:04:55.188 --> 00:04:56.988
Technology is only half of it.

71
00:04:57.038 --> 00:04:58.653
Someone must own the chain.

72
00:04:58.703 --> 00:05:03.956
The data owners answer for the content: whether a price is right, and who corrects it.

73
00:05:04.006 --> 00:05:07.841
Operations runs the daily deliveries and talks to the partners.

74
00:05:07.891 --> 00:05:11.541
And I T looks after the systems, the transfers and security.

75
00:05:11.591 --> 00:05:16.166
When these roles are clear, problems are solved in minutes instead of days.

76
00:05:17.516 --> 00:05:20.586
Some architectures cause trouble sooner or later.

77
00:05:20.636 --> 00:05:24.724
A separate script for every partner, each one a little different.

78
00:05:24.774 --> 00:05:27.270
Manual steps in the middle of the night.

79
00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:30.072
No archive, so nobody can say what was sent.

80
00:05:30.122 --> 00:05:33.382
Checks only at the receiver, so errors travel first.

81
00:05:33.432 --> 00:05:38.157
And no monitoring, so a missing file is noticed by the partner, not by you.

82
00:05:39.507 --> 00:05:40.270
Build or buy?

83
00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:45.146
Some companies build the chain themselves, with a database and scheduled jobs.

84
00:05:45.196 --> 00:05:52.023
Others use the data management or distribution platforms of specialised vendors, many of which speak FundsXML.

85
00:05:52.073 --> 00:05:56.649
A common middle way: a vendor for distribution, and the own checks on top.

86
00:05:56.699 --> 00:05:59.525
Either way, the building blocks stay the same.

87
00:06:00.875 --> 00:06:03.542
Every chain needs at least two environments.

88
00:06:03.592 --> 00:06:10.397
A test environment, where new partners, new versions and changes are tried with example data and test partners.

89
00:06:10.447 --> 00:06:12.805
And production, with the real partners.

90
00:06:12.855 --> 00:06:21.265
Both run the same code, but with separate data, separate keys and separate addresses, so that a test file can never reach a real partner.

91
00:06:22.615 --> 00:06:27.606
Plan the document IDs carefully, because everything downstream relies on them.

92
00:06:27.656 --> 00:06:29.480
An ID must be unique forever.

93
00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:35.683
A simple pattern works well: the supplier, the kind of delivery, the date and a sequence number.

94
00:06:35.733 --> 00:06:40.336
A resend of the same file keeps its ID; a corrected file gets a new one.

95
00:06:40.386 --> 00:06:42.921
Never reuse an ID, not even after years.

96
00:06:44.271 --> 00:06:59.659
For the next films we use a complete example, published in the FundsXML examples repository on GitHub: a PostgreSQL database, a loader for FundsXML files and a generator back to FundsXML, with six sample deliveries and the files it writes for three partners.

97
00:06:59.709 --> 00:07:06.301
It is an example, not a product, but it shows the essential steps, and you can download it and run it yourself.

98
00:07:07.651 --> 00:07:10.413
That was the delivery chain on both sides.

99
00:07:10.463 --> 00:07:14.966
In the next film, you see how FundsXML data is stored in a database.

100
00:07:15.016 --> 00:07:16.305
Thanks for watching.
